1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,440
Hello. A lot of people love the anthem, the Marseillaise, the anthem of France.

2
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:08,500
But have you ever wondered what the backstory of the Marseillaise is?

3
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:10,720
Why it's so drenched in blood?

4
00:00:10,940 --> 00:00:15,200
This is the episode where you will discover everything you ever want to know about the Marseillaise,

5
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:17,840
about ancient Rome, about the way the people dressed in the French Revolution,

6
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:20,580
and about the history of trousers. Don't miss it.

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:26,120
La Marseillaise, the song par excellence of the French Revolution,

8
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:30,120
or at least the one French Revolutionary song that everybody now remembers.

9
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:35,480
So, Tom, that was adopted as the official anthem of the French Republic in 1795,

10
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:37,100
but Napoleon didn't like it.

11
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:39,780
He didn't. He kind of pushed it back to the margins.

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,820
And then after the revolution, after Napoleon, Louis XVI's brothers,

13
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:48,000
Louis XVIII and Charles X, they come back.

14
00:00:48,020 --> 00:00:50,560
They obviously hate it, so they ban it completely.

15
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:55,500
But the Marseillaise is never forgotten, and it ends up as the national anthem of France.

16
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:59,780
Would you, I mean, you are John Bull incarnate,

17
00:00:59,800 --> 00:01:06,500
but would you agree that it's the greatest, the most thrilling national anthem ever written?

18
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:07,680
I mean, the most stirring?

19
00:01:07,940 --> 00:01:12,440
Well, I saw you'd written this, and this is the sort of slack and lazy thing

20
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:15,160
that people who know only two national anthems say.

21
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:17,820
So they know their own, and they know one or two others.

22
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:19,740
If they're American, they say it's the American one.

23
00:01:19,820 --> 00:01:24,380
If they're British, a particular kind of Britain, they say it's the French national anthem.

24
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:26,100
But actually, people who know a lot about national anthems,

25
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:28,100
know that the best national anthem by far,

26
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:31,380
so our assistant producer, Aaliyah, will back me up on this,

27
00:01:31,820 --> 00:01:34,760
is the anthem of the German Democratic Republic,

28
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:39,360
Auf der Standen aus Rhenen, the anthem of East Germany,

29
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:40,860
which was an absolute banger,

30
00:01:40,940 --> 00:01:46,300
and unquestionably the greatest national anthem ever written.

31
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:48,320
Well, Dominic, for those of us who aren't familiar with it,

32
00:01:48,360 --> 00:01:49,440
would you like to sing it for us?

33
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:49,820
No.

34
00:01:50,420 --> 00:01:50,820
No?

35
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:52,080
No, I wouldn't.

36
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:53,880
You're clearly afraid?

37
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:55,960
No, I just don't want to look like an absolute fool.

38
00:01:56,120 --> 00:01:57,340
Ah, I don't think so.

39
00:01:57,520 --> 00:01:59,760
Because obviously, as an Englishman,

40
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,460
my soul doesn't stir with patriotic fervor when I hear the Marseillaise,

41
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:08,780
except in Casablanca, that brilliant scene where

42
00:02:08,780 --> 00:02:09,780
Yeah, they all stand up.

43
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:11,300
the Germans are sat around the piano.

44
00:02:11,580 --> 00:02:11,940
Yeah.

45
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:15,500
And then Humphrey Bogart agrees that they can strike up the Marseillaise,

46
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:17,420
and it's sung with such fervor, and you are

47
00:02:17,420 --> 00:02:18,520
Yeah, you love that.

48
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:21,300
Yeah, identifying with it, and it's very, very stirring.

49
00:02:21,620 --> 00:02:24,040
However, I mean, it is worth mentioning,

50
00:02:24,220 --> 00:02:25,860
for those who haven't looked closely,

51
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:29,980
at the lyrics, that they are incredibly martial.

52
00:02:30,220 --> 00:02:31,360
Yeah, the bloody standard.

53
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:33,520
Yes, I mean, it's kind of blood everywhere.

54
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:33,980
Yeah.

55
00:02:34,060 --> 00:02:37,320
And it is summoning the enfants de la patrie,

56
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:39,640
the children of the fatherland, to action.

57
00:02:39,740 --> 00:02:41,920
In that first verse that Theo sung so beautifully,

58
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:46,020
France is menaced by ferocious soldiers

59
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:51,100
who are marching beneath this banner of tyranny,

60
00:02:51,140 --> 00:02:52,600
which has been steeped in blood,

61
00:02:52,780 --> 00:02:55,700
and they're coming to rip out the throats of,

62
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:58,620
patriotic Frenchmen's sons and wives.

63
00:02:58,980 --> 00:03:01,800
And so this great summons to arms, citizens,

64
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:04,060
form your battalions, let's march, let's march,

65
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:08,020
and irrigate the soil with a tainted blood.

66
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:12,180
And the tainted blood is the blood of the tyrants and their armies.

67
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:14,340
Yeah, that tells you when it's written.

68
00:03:14,520 --> 00:03:20,500
So war has been declared, but it also tells you about the mood at the time.

69
00:03:20,560 --> 00:03:25,680
So we've talked a fair bit about the sort of paranoia, sense of conspiracy, sense of taint,

70
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:29,340
and impurity, and invading the virtuous body of the French Republic.

71
00:03:29,640 --> 00:03:30,780
But tell us a little bit more, Tom.

72
00:03:30,880 --> 00:03:32,280
I know you've done some digging into this.

73
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:32,900
Right.

74
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,080
How it comes to be written, and who by?

75
00:03:35,360 --> 00:03:38,500
Well, it's written at a time when France is still a monarchy, just about.

76
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:41,600
So it's written on the 25th of April, 1792,

77
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:45,440
and that's five days after France has declared war on Austria.

78
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:50,360
And officers in Strasbourg, who are kind of pretty much on the front line,

79
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:53,000
there's a dinner, a public dinner,

80
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:55,660
and it's hosted by the mayor of Strasbourg,

81
00:03:55,660 --> 00:03:57,540
who's a man called Philippe Dietrich.

82
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:04,920
And in one sense, he is the absolute embodiment of the intellectual revolution,

83
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:07,500
the Enlightenment-infused revolution.

84
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:09,080
So he's a Protestant.

85
00:04:09,870 --> 00:04:12,300
He is a distinguished scientist.

86
00:04:12,540 --> 00:04:13,340
He's a chemist.

87
00:04:13,460 --> 00:04:14,680
He's a geologist.

88
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:16,800
He loves his Enlightenment ideals.

89
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:18,180
And he's a Freemason.

90
00:04:18,460 --> 00:04:19,920
Yeah, total bore. Go on.

91
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:23,700
But what makes it intriguing is that he is simultaneously,

92
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:27,940
he is the epitome of everything that the revolution is opposed to,

93
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:30,900
because he is an iron master and an industrialist.

94
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:32,000
Now, I do like an iron master.

95
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:32,940
I know you do.

96
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:39,080
And his forges are being powered by all the timber and the wood that he's been harvesting,

97
00:04:39,180 --> 00:04:43,440
and which the poor can no longer use to keep their pots boiling.

98
00:04:43,700 --> 00:04:44,940
And so they're all starving.

99
00:04:45,080 --> 00:04:48,420
So he's a figure of sinister wealth.

100
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:51,240
And he's actually a former aristocrat.

101
00:04:51,360 --> 00:04:53,600
So he was the Baron de Dietrich.

102
00:04:53,600 --> 00:04:59,740
So he embraces all the contradictions and ambivalences of the revolution.

103
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,120
And it's not surprising that he should be keen on reinvention,

104
00:05:03,180 --> 00:05:07,940
the kind of reinvention that France itself in this period is having to undergo,

105
00:05:08,260 --> 00:05:11,360
the process of going from a monarchy to a republic.

106
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:17,640
And it's, of course, they are on the frontier of a country at war.

107
00:05:17,980 --> 00:05:23,500
And so at dinner, the toasts are offered exactly the kind that you can imagine.

108
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:32,000
So to the atri, to liberty, to the ruin of tyrants, to the fertilizing of the soil with their blood and the blood of tyrants, all of that.

109
00:05:34,240 --> 00:05:42,700
And Dietrich, he finds all the words of these toasts stirring.

110
00:05:42,940 --> 00:05:52,620
And he comments to the table, to the assembled officers, how tragic it is that all the marching songs that the French army have are rife with feudalism and superstition.

111
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:53,020
Yeah.

112
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:55,180
They derive from the Ancien Régime.

113
00:05:55,580 --> 00:06:04,280
And he wishes that there was a marching song that would be appropriate to the new French army, an army of patriots and citizens.

114
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:10,180
And it so happens that at that dinner, there is a captain called Roger de Lisle.

115
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:15,440
And he is almost a kind of mini-Dietrich because he's a man of science.

116
00:06:15,480 --> 00:06:17,180
He's an engineer. He's a freemason.

117
00:06:17,380 --> 00:06:19,940
But he's also a part-time composer.

118
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:22,540
So he spent quite a lot of time in Paris rustling up

119
00:06:22,540 --> 00:06:29,340
various forgettable songs that he's trying to kind of flog to theaters and so on.

120
00:06:29,420 --> 00:06:34,340
But that night, the muse descends on him and he's inspired by Dietrich's suggestion.

121
00:06:34,620 --> 00:06:43,880
He goes back to his quarters and he spends all night writing a song that he very catchily calls the Song of the Army of the Rhine.

122
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:50,660
And I think that if that, you know, if it had stayed, that had stayed its title, maybe it wouldn't be as successful as it proves to be.

123
00:06:50,820 --> 00:06:52,520
And the next morning, he goes to the Dietrich.

124
00:06:52,520 --> 00:06:58,860
And he gives it to Dietrich himself, who's very, very taken with it.

125
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:00,220
And Dietrich's musical.

126
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:05,720
And his wife sits down at the piano, plays the chords, and Dietrich bellows it out.

127
00:07:06,200 --> 00:07:08,780
And they all say, this is amazing.

128
00:07:08,940 --> 00:07:10,360
This is exactly what we need.

129
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:14,640
And so Madame Dietrich scores it for a military band.

130
00:07:15,220 --> 00:07:18,660
And the band strikes up.

131
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:21,820
And it's kind of literally...

132
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:23,160
A revelation.

133
00:07:23,640 --> 00:07:31,860
Because to quote David Andres in his wonderful book on the terror, he describes it as an almost millennial sense of drama.

134
00:07:32,120 --> 00:07:36,320
And there is that sense, I think, in which it's a kind of secular dies irae.

135
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:46,640
Dies irae, you know, the great medieval account of souls being brought before the throne of God to be judged amid slaughter and chaos and bloodshed.

136
00:07:46,780 --> 00:07:50,700
And the lamb, the sheep and the goats are separated out.

137
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:51,820
And this essentially...

138
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:56,520
Is what is happening in the drama of what will come to be called the Marseillaise.

139
00:07:56,720 --> 00:07:58,600
Well, it has that apocalyptic sense, right?

140
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:01,660
That is so common in the political culture at this point.

141
00:08:01,720 --> 00:08:02,700
So 1792.

142
00:08:02,740 --> 00:08:09,380
I read with great relish that the Marseillaise music is probably not exactly plagiarized, but it's derivative.

143
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:11,720
So there are various antecedents.

144
00:08:11,780 --> 00:08:13,800
There was an Italian composer called Viotti.

145
00:08:14,280 --> 00:08:17,320
There was an oratoria by somebody called Grison.

146
00:08:18,240 --> 00:08:19,280
And Mozart.

147
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:21,620
Mozart piano concerto.

148
00:08:21,620 --> 00:08:27,140
Number 25, apparently, is disturbingly similar, Tom.

149
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:34,840
Well, remember that de Lisle has come to Strasbourg from Paris where he's been stationed and he's been hawking his stuff around all the concert halls and theatres.

150
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:38,860
He's got a knockoff tune that he's trying to pass off to Dietrich.

151
00:08:39,140 --> 00:08:48,180
I guess in the way that minor composers, I gather, are often more prone to being influenced by things that they've heard than perhaps the very greatest composers.

152
00:08:48,300 --> 00:08:49,720
And he's clearly not a very great composer.

153
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:51,160
But it wouldn't be surprising.

154
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:56,000
That he might perhaps unconsciously plagiarize certain tunes that he's heard.

155
00:08:56,120 --> 00:08:58,220
Well, I mean, that's how musical composition works.

156
00:08:58,400 --> 00:08:58,600
Yeah.

157
00:08:58,700 --> 00:09:10,700
But the bottom line is that it starts to kind of go viral because soldiers from Strasbourg, whenever, you know, they various reasons they might be going back to into the heartlands of France and they take it with them.

158
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:11,940
They start kind of singing it.

159
00:09:12,020 --> 00:09:20,060
And one of the soldiers who does this is going to Montpellier in the south of France and there on the 17th of June.

160
00:09:20,080 --> 00:09:20,480
Um.

161
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:32,840
A doctor by the name of François Mireux hears it being sung by a soldier who's come from Strasbourg and he immediately recognizes it as something stirring, as something patriotic.

162
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:37,360
And he finds himself humming it and then singing it.

163
00:09:37,460 --> 00:09:45,400
He gets the lyrics and five days later he is going to a meeting at the Jacobin headquarters in Marseille.

164
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:46,720
So he himself is a Jacobin.

165
00:09:46,820 --> 00:09:48,780
He's a great enthusiast for the revolution.

166
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:50,360
And he goes to the.

167
00:09:50,360 --> 00:10:03,360
Jacobin club in Marseille and he gives a very fiery speech and brilliantly he ends it by bursting into song and singing this, this, this tremendous song that he's heard from the soldier from Strasbourg and everyone at the club goes, yeah, this is fantastic.

168
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:08,520
And ask for the words to be written down and to be printed and to be distributed.

169
00:10:09,020 --> 00:10:10,140
And quick question.

170
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:12,120
How does he know the song?

171
00:10:12,280 --> 00:10:15,040
Like there are multiple, multiple verses.

172
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:16,380
How has he learned it?

173
00:10:16,440 --> 00:10:17,320
He must have got it.

174
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:20,260
He must have got the lyrics from the soldier and the soldier must have got the lyrics.

175
00:10:20,260 --> 00:10:22,580
Cause they've been printed off in Strasbourg.

176
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:23,380
Oh, they've been printed.

177
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:23,640
Okay.

178
00:10:23,760 --> 00:10:24,000
Yeah.

179
00:10:24,060 --> 00:10:26,480
So I assume that that's how it's been propagated.

180
00:10:27,320 --> 00:10:31,660
And Miller is, I mean, he is, he is a hot Jacobin.

181
00:10:31,980 --> 00:10:50,240
So we talked about how this great summons went out from the capital to enthusiasts for the revolution out in the provinces to come and celebrate the, the, the FET de la federation, which has begun to be celebrated annually on the, the anniversary of the storming of the revolution.

182
00:10:50,260 --> 00:10:54,080
Of the Bastille on the 14th of July on the Chambre de Mars in Paris.

183
00:10:54,500 --> 00:11:05,360
And so the people who come from the provinces to Paris to, to supplement the, uh, the, the, the guards that are available to the revolutionary authorities in Paris come to be called Federer.

184
00:11:05,580 --> 00:11:05,760
Yeah.

185
00:11:05,840 --> 00:11:09,840
And Mira has already enrolled himself as a Federer by this point.

186
00:11:10,380 --> 00:11:19,860
And, um, he is part of a band of about 500 volunteers who've been recruited in the south and who are going to leave from Marseille.

187
00:11:19,860 --> 00:11:33,180
And go to Paris to join the other Federer from across France and Mira is appointed to the general staff of this contingent of Federer who are going to march north and they duly leave on the 2nd of July.

188
00:11:33,640 --> 00:11:35,640
They are armed to the teeth.

189
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:39,880
They are dragging artillery with them, heading off to Paris.

190
00:11:40,060 --> 00:11:49,740
And as they march, they sing this song and the name that had been given to the song by, um, by, by Delille, the song of the army of the Ryan is obviously not suitable.

191
00:11:49,740 --> 00:11:52,440
To a contingent of men coming from the south of France.

192
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:57,320
So they change it to the even more catchily named the war song of the armies on the borders.

193
00:11:58,620 --> 00:11:59,520
And it's great.

194
00:11:59,700 --> 00:12:05,740
And they marched up through from Marseille up towards Paris and everywhere they go, people absolutely love the song.

195
00:12:05,940 --> 00:12:10,520
And by the time they come to Paris, I think that news of the song has preceded them.

196
00:12:10,660 --> 00:12:12,560
And so they march into the city.

197
00:12:12,860 --> 00:12:19,320
And the sound of their singing is electrifying for a city that is already.

198
00:12:19,340 --> 00:12:19,720
Um,

199
00:12:19,720 --> 00:12:27,520
On edge, excited, nervous in a kind of apocalyptic mood, fearing the worst, hoping for the best.

200
00:12:27,620 --> 00:12:30,260
And it just strikes this incredible cord.

201
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:44,780
And it's a reminder of the fact that at this point with France poised between monarchy and a republic, there is a need for anthems, for new anthems that can channel identity in a way that previous anthems hadn't.

202
00:12:44,820 --> 00:12:49,060
So if you think of, you know, the British national anthem, it's God save the king.

203
00:12:49,060 --> 00:12:57,120
It's focused on the monarchy, but if you're going to get rid of a monarchy, you need new songs, new tunes, but you know, what's interesting about it though.

204
00:12:57,180 --> 00:13:04,420
It's not entirely uncontested because in the next few months, there are lots of people who don't like the Marseillaise and who you actually use it as a stick to beat other people with.

205
00:13:04,540 --> 00:13:18,980
So I read in Peter McPhee's great history of the French revolution that as the tension, the few between the Girondins and the Jacobin became more intense, the Girondins actually had a parody of the Marseillaise that they used as a kind of, they distributed that.

206
00:13:18,980 --> 00:13:24,420
Or they did that later that year as a Christmas Carol by a guy called Antoine Joseph-Gorsas.

207
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:32,720
And it began, forward children of anarchy, the shameful day is upon us, the people blinded by their rage, raise the bloody knife.

208
00:13:32,960 --> 00:13:43,660
So it actually turns all that stuff about blood on its head and makes it a song about anarchy and basically tarring the Jacobin as these sort of blood crazed fiends.

209
00:13:44,240 --> 00:13:48,960
I mean, I think that an anxiety about the sanguinary quality of the lyrics has never been.

210
00:13:48,980 --> 00:13:53,560
never entirely gone away. I mean, it's still, there are still occasional proposals in France

211
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:59,720
to this day that the lyrics perhaps should be rewritten to be slightly less carnivorous.

212
00:13:59,880 --> 00:14:05,480
But conversely, it's the fact that it is so aggressive that means that people who are

213
00:14:05,480 --> 00:14:09,760
caught up in the excitement of this new stage of the revolution, the second revolution,

214
00:14:10,040 --> 00:14:15,200
as you called it in previous episodes, that's exactly what you want. You want something

215
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:20,800
that is no holds barred. And this song that comes to be called the Marseillais after these

216
00:14:20,800 --> 00:14:27,740
people from Marseille, the Marseillais, clearly, I mean, it's absolutely what people on the

217
00:14:27,740 --> 00:14:33,760
more radical fringes of the revolution want. And I think that it's not just the lyrics,

218
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:40,120
it's not just the tune. It's also the fact of who is singing it. So they're not professional

219
00:14:40,520 --> 00:14:45,120
soldiers. They're not people who you would

220
00:14:45,120 --> 00:14:51,600
expect under normal circumstances to be singing a marching song. These are citizens who are

